            <ArticleTitle>Peritoneal fluid interleukin-6 in women with chronic pelvic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>325-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the relationship between peritoneal fluid concentrations of interleukin-6 (IL-6) and chronic pelvic pain symptomatology in women with adhesions, endometriosis, or no obvious intraperitoneal pathology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical research study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Healthy volunteers in an academic research environment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Reproductive-aged women undergoing laparoscopy for the diagnosis of pelvic pain, infertility, or sterilization were selected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Peritoneal fluid was collected at the time of the laparoscopy and later assayed for IL-6. Subjects completed a pelvic pain questionnaire, and operative reports were used to obtain the underlying diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Interleukin-6 concentrations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No correlation between the presence or absence of pelvic pain, findings of adhesions or endometriosis, and the concentration of peritoneal fluid IL-6 was observed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The cytokine IL-6 does not seem to play a role in the genesis of chronic pelvic pain in women with adhesions or endometriosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
